      <div class="kpi-strip" data-layer="fact">
        {event.kpis.map((k) => (
          <div class="kpi">
            <span class="kpi-value">{k.value}<small>{k.unit ? ` ${k.unit}` : ""}</small></span>
            <span class="kpi-label">{k.label}</span>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      <aside class="three-things" data-layer="fact">
        <p class="tt-label">Three things to know</p>
        <ol>
          <li>On July 20, 2026, President Donald J. Trump signed three proclamations under Section 338 of the Tariff Act of 1930 on listed goods of Canada. CBP created separate entry headings for goods excluded from the additional Section 338 charge. Those goods carry a 0 percent Section 338 add-on under 9903.03.15 and 9903.03.16 and may remain subject to Section 232, antidumping, countervailing, or other duties. Headings 9903.03.12 through 9903.03.14 carry a 50 percent additional duty.<Cite s="wh-fact-sheet-2026-07-20" passage="three Proclamations pursuant to Section 338 of the Tariff Act of 1930" /><Cite s="cbp-csms-69606660-2026-08-21" passage="shall continue to be subject to antidumping, countervailing, or other duties" /></li>
          <li>Finance Canada's August 26 list of {listItems} tariff items is the union of the two Canadian orders: {oic0785Items} codes in PC 2026-0785 at 15, 25, and 50 percent, and {oic0786Items} steel and aluminum codes in PC 2026-0786 at 25 and 50 percent, with no overlap.<Cite s="finance-product-list-2026-08-26" passage="List updated as of August 26, 2026" /><Cite s="oic-pc-2026-0786" passage="This Order comes into force on the day on which the United States Surtax Order (2026) comes into force" /></li>
          <li>The same Tuesday the Canadian order came into force, the United States signed proclamations that exclude listed Canadian dairy, motorcycle, and alcoholic-beverage lines from importation from September 29, changed those duty baskets from September 15, and, in a White House fact sheet, directed the General Services Administration to take US${gsaDirectedUsd} billion of Canadian-origin products off the catalogues federal agencies buy from.<Cite s="proc-exclude-dairy-2026-09-08" passage="12:01 a.m. eastern time on September 29, 2026" /><Cite s="wh-fact-sheet-2026-09-08" passage="remove $50 billion dollars" /></li>
        </ol>
      </aside>
      <p class="railed rail-note" data-layer="fact">Story updated {event.updated}.</p>

      <div data-layer="proof" class="event-record-context">
        <p class="sources-line">This story rests on the White House fact sheets of July 20 and September 8, the three Section 338 proclamations and their July 20 annex PDFs, Proclamation 11056 as published in the Federal Register, the motor-vehicles proclamation's public-inspection PDF, CBP cargo-systems message 69606660 of August 21, 19 U.S.C. 1338, Prime Minister Carney's August 22 remarks, the Department of Finance Canada release of August 25 and the product tables of August 25 and August 26, the August 27 Sarnia Finance release, the Spring Economic Update 2026 intro, Gazette SOR/2025-181, the Bank of Canada USD/CAD rate for August 25, staff analytical note 2025-18, and the September 2 rate decision, the Employment and Social Development Canada release of August 28, Orders in Council PC 2026-0785 and PC 2026-0786, Canada Border Services Agency Customs Notices 26-23 and 25-11, the motor-vehicle remission order in the Canada Gazette, the Census Bureau's goods-with-Canada series, Statistics Canada's Daily for December 2025, the USTR Canada page and USTR's July 20, February 20, and November 2023 statements, the 2021 USMCA dairy panel report, CRS report R48435, the Supreme Court's February 20, 2026 opinion in No. 24-1287, the White House August 25 reply, the September 8 White House proclamations including the alcohol-basket scope change and their annex PDFs, the White House presidential-actions listings of September 8 and September 9, the Federal Register public-inspection page of September 9, Carney's September 8 words as carried by the Winnipeg Free Press from the YouTube transcript and the YouTube watch page for that video, the June 15 Prime Minister readout on the Marconi SAFE contract, the Major Projects Office projects page, the Quebec government release of September 7, Global Affairs Canada's CUSMA joint-review page and May 2026 monthly trade report, and coverage checked against those records. Days, item counts, and dollar conversions are counted from those files.</p>
      </div>

      <section id="what-happened">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The U.S. proclamations first, then Canada's order</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2>

        <p data-layer="narrative">On July 20, 2026, Trump signed three proclamations under Section 338 of the Tariff Act of 1930. A proclamation here is a presidential order that changes the duty, the extra charge collected at the border, on named goods. Section 338 lets the president add a duty of up to 50 percent when he finds that another country discriminates against U.S. commerce. The three proclamations named baskets of Canadian goods, one framed around alcoholic beverages, one around dairy, and one around motor vehicles, and an additional 50 percent duty on listed lines, "regardless of whether a good originates under the U.S.-Mexico-Canada Agreement (USMCA)."<Cite s="wh-fact-sheet-2026-07-20" passage="regardless of whether a good originates under the U.S.-Mexico-Canada Agreement" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The original effective time in those proclamations was 12:01 a.m. eastern on August 19, 2026.<Cite s="proc-alcohol-2026-07-20" passage="12:01 a.m. eastern time on August 19, 2026" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The July 20 fact sheet said that from March 2025 through February 2026, Canadian imports of U.S. alcoholic beverages "decreased by about 81%, or $582 million," and that "All but two Canadian provinces and territories have halted the purchase, distribution, or retailing of U.S. alcoholic beverages."<Cite s="wh-fact-sheet-2026-07-20" passage="decreased by about 81%, or $582 million" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">On August 18 he signed Proclamation 11056, which moved that effective time to 12:01 a.m. eastern on August 22. A later proclamation of September 8 records that the three-day pause then lapsed and the 50 percent duties "became effective."<Cite s="proc-exclude-dairy-2026-09-08" passage="the additional ad valorem duties imposed under Proclamation 11047 became effective" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">CBP's cargo-systems message of August 21 tells filers which extra-duty heading to use. A heading here is a numbered line in the U.S. tariff schedule.<Cite s="cbp-csms-69606660-2026-08-21" passage="GUIDANCE: Section 338 Additional Duties on Certain Goods of Canada" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">CBP created separate entry headings for goods excluded from the additional Section 338 charge. Headings 9903.03.12, 9903.03.13, and 9903.03.14 each carry a 50 percent additional ad valorem rate of duty.<Cite s="cbp-csms-69606660-2026-08-21" passage="9903.03.12: Articles the product of Canada as provided in subdivision (b)(1) of U.S. note 51" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Headings 9903.03.15 and 9903.03.16 carry a 0 percent Section 338 add-on. Those goods may remain subject to Section 232, antidumping, countervailing, or other duties. Goods under those headings "shall continue to be subject to antidumping, countervailing, or other duties."<Cite s="cbp-csms-69606660-2026-08-21" passage="shall continue to be subject to antidumping, countervailing, or other duties" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The same August 22, Carney told Canadians he had suspended the talks the evening before. He said Canada would match the new U.S. tariffs "dollar for dollar."<Cite s="carney-remarks-2026-08-22" passage="match Washington’s new tariffs dollar for dollar" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">He said the Canadian measures would be concentrated in steel, dairy, appliances, agricultural equipment, pulp and paper, and electronics, and "will come into force the Tuesday after Labour Day." That Tuesday was September 8.<Cite s="carney-remarks-2026-08-22" passage="the Tuesday after Labour Day" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">He said Canada took the step "reluctantly," because "it will raise costs and reduce choice for Canadians."<Cite s="carney-remarks-2026-08-22" passage="raise costs and reduce choice for Canadians" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">On August 25, François-Philippe Champagne, Minister of Finance and National Revenue, with Mélanie Joly, Minister of Industry, and colleagues, put numbers on that claim.<Cite s="finance-countermeasures-2026-08-25" passage="dollar for dollar, rate for rate" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">A tariff, in this story, is the extra charge on an imported good, stated as a percent of its value.<Cite s="finance-countermeasures-2026-08-25" passage="counter-tariffs of 15, 25 and 50 per cent" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Finance Canada called the Canadian reply "counter-tariffs" and "countermeasures": extra charges meant to answer the U.S. duties. The release said the United States had imposed a 50 percent tariff on ${financeCoveredCad} billion of Canadian goods effective August 22.<Cite s="finance-countermeasures-2026-08-25" passage="a 50 per cent tariff on $27.6 billion of Canadian goods" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Finance said Canada would "match the new U.S. tariffs dollar for dollar, rate for rate," with 15, 25, and 50 percent charges on products "covering ${financeCoveredCad} billion in imports from the U.S." It also named a ${supportPackage} billion support package for workers and businesses.<Cite s="finance-countermeasures-2026-08-25" passage="dollar for dollar, rate for rate" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Finance Canada writes in Canadian dollars. The Bank of Canada's USD/CAD daily average on August 25 was {fxUsdCad20260825}, the value of one U.S. dollar in Canadian dollars. C${financeCoveredCad} billion divided by that rate is US${computed.usdCovered} billion, about US${computed.usdCoveredRounded} billion as rounded in the headline. C${supportPackage} billion divided by the same rate is US${computed.usdSupport} billion.<Cite s="boc-fxusdcad-2026-08-25" passage="1.3839" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">BBC the same day converted that coverage as {bbcCovered}, and printed the support as {bbcSupport}.<Cite s="bbc-canada-tariffs-2026-08-25" passage="nearly C$28bn ($20bn; £15bn)" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The Detroit News printed {detroitCovered}.<Cite s="detroitnews-canada-tariffs-2026-08-25" passage="C$27.6 billion ($19.94 billion)" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">BBC wrote that the Conservative opposition asked that the full text of the draft deal with the United States be released.<Cite s="bbc-canada-tariffs-2026-08-25" passage="the Conservative opposition asking that the full text of the draft deal with the US be released" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The White House the same day said the United States had offered Canada "the most preferential market access of any country on Earth."<Cite s="wh-ending-free-ride-2026-08-25" passage="the most preferential market access of any country on Earth" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The same White House release said, "Instead of partnership, Canada chose unreasonable demands, walk-backs, and flat-out rejection."<Cite s="wh-ending-free-ride-2026-08-25" passage="Instead of partnership, Canada chose unreasonable demands, walk-backs, and flat-out rejection" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Carney, on August 22, listed what Canada had offered in the talks that then stopped. Canada was willing to drop remaining retaliatory tariffs on steel, aluminum, and autos if the United States substantially lowered its own, to encourage the provinces to return U.S. alcohol to the shelves, and to take administrative measures to protect supply management without changing the system, the U.S. quotas, or the tariffs that would apply. It was not prepared to compromise Canada's sovereignty, the protection of the French language and culture, or what it called key industries. Those issues, he said, "were never on the table."<Cite s="carney-remarks-2026-08-22" passage="were never on the table" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The three July 20 proclamations state the U.S. grievances in those files. The alcohol proclamation names provincial liquor boards that halted the purchase, distribution, or retailing of U.S. alcoholic beverages. The September 8 alcohol-ban proclamation adds that Saskatchewan, on August 27, announced an additional 50 percent levy on U.S. alcoholic beverages, effective September 8. The dairy proclamation names Canada's tariff-rate quota allocation for cheeses under the United States-Mexico-Canada Agreement as compared with the Canada-European Union Comprehensive Economic and Trade Agreement. A 2021 USMCA panel found Canada's practice of reserving those quota pools exclusively for processors inconsistent with the treaty. In 2023 a panel majority found that Canada's revised measures "are not inconsistent with the USMCA provisions cited by the United States." The 2026 proclamation's comparison with the European agreement is an executive finding. No panel has ruled on that comparison. The motor-vehicles proclamation names Canada's motor vehicle tariff scheme. The U.S. draft terms themselves are not a saved record on this page.<Cite s="proc-exclude-alcohol-2026-09-08" passage="additional 50 percent levy on U.S. alcoholic beverages" /><Cite s="usmca-dairy-panel-2021" passage="exclusively for the use of processors is inconsistent with Canada’s commitment" /><Cite s="ustr-dairy-panel-2023-11-24" passage="Canada’s measures are not inconsistent with the USMCA provisions cited by the United States" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">A tariff item is a line in the customs tariff, the numbered list used to classify a good at the border. The Finance table as saved from an Internet Archive copy of August 25 lists {listItemsAug25} such lines, including the whole fish and seafood category, chapter 03, from live fish onward.<Cite s="finance-product-list-2026-08-25" passage="These countermeasures will be effective as of 12:01 a.m., September 8, 2026." /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The table "updated as of August 26, 2026" lists {listItems} lines at those three rates. Overnight, every one of the {removedCodes} fish and seafood lines came off. {addedCodes} industrial lines went on: wood charcoal, printed pictures, gypsum board, glass containers, and copper wire, all at 50 percent. The public count fell by {listCountDrop}. Finance's coverage figure stayed C${financeCoveredCad} billion. Neither table names a reason. An Employment and Social Development Canada release dated August 28, two days after the table changed, still listed fish and seafood among the goods facing 25 percent tariffs.<Cite s="finance-product-list-2026-08-26" passage="4402.90.10 | Wood charcoal" /><Cite s="finance-countermeasures-2026-08-25" passage="products covering $27.6 billion in imports from the U.S." /><Cite s="esdc-countermeasures-2026-08-28" passage="fish and seafood" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">An Order in Council is a cabinet order, here made by the Governor General on the finance minister's recommendation. PC 2026-0785, dated September 4, makes the United States Surtax Order (2026). A surtax is the Canadian legal name for that extra charge. The order sets 15 percent on Schedule 1, 25 percent on Schedule 2, and 50 percent on Schedule 3, and says it "comes into force on September 8, 2026."<Cite s="oic-pc-2026-0785" passage="comes into force on September 8, 2026" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The Canadian package is two coordinated orders. English Schedules 1 to 3 of PC 2026-0785 list {oic0785Items} tariff items ({oic0785Schedule1} at 15 percent, {oic0785Schedule2} at 25 percent, {oic0785Schedule3} at 50 percent). PC 2026-0786, made the same day, is the Order Amending the United States Surtax Order (Steel and Aluminum 2025). Its English Schedules 1, 1.1, 2, and 2.1 list {oic0786Items} unique steel and aluminum codes ({oic0786Schedule1} plus {oic0786Schedule2} at 25 percent, {oic0786Schedule11} plus {oic0786Schedule21} at 50 percent). No code sits on both orders. Their union is {oicUnionItems} unique codes, the same {listItems} unique codes as the Finance August 26 table, and every Finance last-column rate matches the schedule that code sits in. CBSA Customs Notice 25-11 names that amending order SOR/2026-187. The captured order text does not print that number.<Cite s="oic-pc-2026-0786" passage="This Order comes into force on the day on which the United States Surtax Order (2026) comes into force" /><Cite s="cbsa-cn25-11" passage="Order Amending the United States Surtax Order (Steel and Aluminum 2025)" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Finance Canada's product-list page, not the Order, says the countermeasures "will be effective as of 12:01 a.m., September 8, 2026."<Cite s="finance-product-list-2026-08-26" passage="These countermeasures will be effective as of 12:01 a.m., September 8, 2026." /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The Canada Border Services Agency, which collects the charge, issued Customs Notice 26-23 on September 7 explaining how it applies, including that goods "in transit to Canada" that day are not charged.<Cite s="cbsa-cn26-23" passage="in transit to Canada" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The same Tuesday the Canadian order came into force, Trump signed proclamations that exclude certain Canadian products from importation. An exclusion here is a ban: Section 338 lets the president bar a country's goods entirely if he finds the discrimination continued. The dairy proclamation states that rule in paragraph 9. The bans take effect at 12:01 a.m. eastern on September 29. A further proclamation changes the motor-vehicles basket from September 15: {annexAutoAddItems} HTSUS lines added at 50 percent, {annexAutoRemoveItems} lines taken off that duty. Another of the same day changes the alcoholic-beverages basket from September 15: {annexAlcoholScopeAddItems} HTSUS lines added at 50 percent, including specified cheeses, hides, furskins, and motorboats, and {annexAlcoholScopeRemoveItems} whisky and liqueur lines taken off that duty.<Cite s="proc-modify-alcohol-2026-09-08" passage="12:01 a.m. eastern time on September 15, 2026" /></p>

        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>From the U.S. proclamations to Canada's order</strong><span>Dates from the White House proclamations and annexes, Carney's remarks, Finance Canada, the Order in Council, and CBSA.</span></figcaption>
          <DecisionTimeline entries={timeline} />
          <p class="chart-source">Seventeen days separate the U.S. duties taking effect on August 22 and Canada's order taking effect on September 8.<Cite s="oic-pc-2026-0785" passage="comes into force on September 8, 2026" /></p>
        </figure>

        <QuoteCard when="August 22, 2026" where="Remarks on Canada-U.S. trade negotiations" label="Prime Minister of Canada">
          <p>Canada will match Washington’s new tariffs dollar for dollar in order to protect Canadian workers, farmers, families, and businesses.<Cite s="carney-remarks-2026-08-22" passage="match Washington’s new tariffs dollar for dollar" /></p>
        </QuoteCard>

        <QuoteCard when="August 25, 2026" where="Department of Finance Canada news release" label="Finance Canada">
          <p>When the United States asked too much and offered too little, we chose to stand up for Canadians.<Cite s="finance-countermeasures-2026-08-25" passage="When the United States asked too much and offered too little, we chose to stand up for Canadians" /></p>
        </QuoteCard>

        <QuoteCard when="September 8, 2026" where="Forward Guidance video, as transcribed by the Winnipeg Free Press" label="Winnipeg Free Press, from the YouTube transcript">
          <p>I don’t believe in escalating the conflict, that’s not constructive, but our tariffs are necessary to protect our workers, companies and communities.<Cite s="wfp-carney-forward-guidance-2026-09-08" passage="I don’t believe in escalating the conflict, that’s not constructive" /></p>
        </QuoteCard>

        <SourcedBlock source="oic-pc-2026-0785" kind="record" detail>
          <p>PC 2026-0785: goods originating in the United States classified under Schedule 1, 2, or 3 "are subject to a surtax" of 15, 25, or 50 percent of the value for duty. Goods "in transit to Canada on the day on which this Order comes into force" are excepted.<Cite s="oic-pc-2026-0785" passage="in transit to Canada on the day on which this Order comes into force" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="cbsa-cn26-23" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Customs Notice 26-23, Ottawa, September 7, 2026: effective September 8, listed U.S.-origin goods "are subject to a surtax of either 15%, 25% or 50% of the value for duty." Origin is the CUSMA marking rule. Remission is a write-off of a surtax "paid or payable," so it can stop a payment or refund one. The 2025 remission order and the U.S. Remission Framework remain available.<Cite s="cbsa-cn26-23" passage="surtax of either 15%, 25% or 50% of the value for duty" /><Cite s="oic-pc-2026-0785" passage="remission is granted of surtaxes paid or payable" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="the-catch">
        <aside class="the-catch" data-layer="fact">
          <p class="tt-label">The catch</p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <h3>The count fell. The dollar did not.</h3>
              The public list shrank by {listCountDrop} lines when every fish and seafood tariff item came off and nine industrial lines went on. Finance's C${financeCoveredCad} billion did not move. Why it matters: Finance still assigns the same covered-trade figure after the whole seafood category left the table, and no saved record shows the import-value calculation by tariff code and reference period.<Cite s="finance-product-list-2026-08-26" passage="List updated as of August 26, 2026" /><Cite s="finance-countermeasures-2026-08-25" passage="products covering $27.6 billion in imports from the U.S." /> <a class="catch-jump" href="#claim-check">Claim checked</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>A duty, then a ban, the same Tuesday.</h3>
              Finance says Canada answered a 50 percent U.S. duty on listed goods. The same day, the United States signed proclamations that turn part of its own 50 percent duty into an import ban from September 29: {annexDairyBanItems} dairy-basket lines including whey, {annexMotorBanItems} motorcycle line, and {annexAlcoholBanItems} alcoholic-beverage lines. It also changed the alcoholic-beverages basket from September 15: {annexAlcoholScopeAddItems} HTSUS lines added at 50 percent, {annexAlcoholScopeRemoveItems} taken off. Why it matters: a Canadian exporter of the banned lines does not pay a higher U.S. duty. The goods are barred. The added alcohol-basket lines stay dutiable, at 50 percent, from September 15.<Cite s="annex-i-alcohol-scope-2026-09-08" passage="New Products Covered by Section 338 Tariffs" /> <a class="catch-jump" href="#who-feels-it">Who feels it</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>The U.S. 50 percent baskets include 0 percent headings.</h3>
              CBP created separate entry headings for goods excluded from the additional Section 338 charge. Headings 9903.03.12 through 9903.03.14 carry a 50 percent additional duty. Headings 9903.03.15 and 9903.03.16 carry a 0 percent Section 338 add-on and may remain subject to Section 232, antidumping, countervailing, or other duties. Why it matters: Finance's rate-for-rate claim is not a claim against every good in those three baskets.<Cite s="cbp-csms-69606660-2026-08-21" passage="9903.03.16: Articles of civil aircraft" /> <a class="catch-jump" href="#what-happened">What happened</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>The fight reaches federal catalogues.</h3>
              The White House fact sheet of September 8 says the President directed USTR and GSA to remove US${gsaDirectedUsd} billion of Canadian-origin products from Multiple Award Schedules, the catalogues federal agencies buy from. No saved record shows a removal carried out. Why it matters: the confrontation is no longer only a border duty. It now names government purchasing, at a claimed scale larger than Finance's C${financeCoveredCad} billion list.<Cite s="wh-fact-sheet-2026-09-08" passage="remove $50 billion dollars" /> <a class="catch-jump" href="#what-happened-next">What happened next</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>The Canadian charge is at Canada's border.</h3>
              Carney said the step "will raise costs and reduce choice for Canadians."<Cite s="carney-remarks-2026-08-22" passage="raise costs and reduce choice for Canadians" /> CBSA makes the importer declare the surtax. The Bank of Canada's 2018 staff note found "high but incomplete pass-through of tariffs to consumer goods prices." Its April 2025 scenarios "assume that 75% of the increased costs from tariffs is passed on to consumer prices within six quarters." Those are a finding and an assumption, not the same thing. The September 2 decision said the measures "could feed into consumer prices over time." Why it matters: the charge is due from the importer unless an exception or remission stops it. Whether a household pays more is not measured here.<Cite s="cbsa-cn26-23" passage="Importers must declare imported goods as subject to a surtax" /><Cite s="boc-san-2025-18" passage="high but incomplete pass-through of tariffs to consumer goods prices" /><Cite s="boc-fad-2026-09-02" passage="could feed into consumer prices over time" /> <a class="catch-jump" href="#who-feels-it">Who feels it</a>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="where-this-sits">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Two annual trade totals, and a three-rate list</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Where this sits</h2>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The Census Bureau's goods-with-Canada series, saved from the country page, puts 2025 U.S. goods exports to Canada at US${computed.censusExports2025Rounded} billion and U.S. goods imports from Canada at US${computed.censusImports2025Rounded} billion. The USTR Canada page states the same 2025 goods-export and goods-import totals as $333.6 billion and $381.9 billion.<Cite s="census-c1220-canada" passage="TOTAL 2025" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Statistics Canada's Daily for December 2025, on a balance-of-payments basis, says 2025 exports to the United States were down 5.8 percent and imports from the United States decreased 2.9 percent, leaving a merchandise trade surplus with the United States of $81.6 billion. It says the United States took 71.7 percent of Canada's merchandise exports in 2025, down from 75.9 percent in 2024, and supplied 58.8 percent of imports, down from 62.3 percent.<Cite s="statcan-daily-2025-12" passage="merchandise trade surplus with the United States totalled $81.6 billion" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Finance Canada's C${financeCoveredCad} billion is the figure the department uses for the slice of goods it says is covered, about US${computed.usdCoveredRounded} billion at the August 25 Bank of Canada rate.<Cite s="finance-countermeasures-2026-08-25" passage="products covering $27.6 billion in imports from the U.S." /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">USTR on July 20 valued the three Section 338 actions at nearly US$20 billion in imports from Canada.<Cite s="ustr-section-338-2026-07-20" passage="nearly $20 billion in imports from Canada" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The White House July 20 fact sheet names the 50 percent rate, Section 338, and the three baskets. It does not name a dollar value for the goods those proclamations cover.<Cite s="wh-fact-sheet-2026-07-20" passage="three Proclamations pursuant to Section 338 of the Tariff Act of 1930" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Canada's Spring Economic Update 2026, written before the July 20 duties, said "approximately 85% of our goods trade tariff-free."<Cite s="budget-seu-2026-intro" passage="With approximately 85% of our goods trade tariff-free" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Chart 0.7's text version prints "Average Tariff Rate Canada 5.2 Global 11.4." That is the state the update described, not the state after September 8.<Cite s="budget-seu-2026-intro" passage="Average Tariff Rate" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The Congressional Research Service, writing on February 27, 2025, said that as of that writing the United States had never imposed tariffs under Section 338.<Cite s="crs-r48435" passage="As of the time of this writing, the United States has never imposed tariffs under" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The statute itself says the new duties are levied "thirty days after the date of such proclamation." The July 20 proclamations set that clock at August 19, {daysJulyProcToOriginalEffective} days later. The September 8 modifications make the newly added lines dutiable from September 15, {daysSeptModifyToEffective} days after those proclamations, citing section 338 and section 604 of the Trade Act of 1974. No court has ruled on a Section 338 duty. The seven-day interval is a count a reader can check from those dates. The proclamations rest on the statute's power to amend an earlier proclamation. This page does not call that interval unlawful.<Cite s="usc-19-1338" passage="thirty days after the date of such proclamation there shall be levied, collected, and paid upon the articles enumerated in such proclamation" /><Cite s="proc-alcohol-2026-07-20" passage="12:01 a.m. eastern time on August 19, 2026" /><Cite s="proc-modify-motor-2026-09-08" passage="12:01 a.m. eastern time on September 15, 2026" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The Supreme Court held on February 20, 2026, in Learning Resources v. Trump, No. 24-1287, that the International Emergency Economic Powers Act, a 1977 statute the administration had used for other tariffs, does not authorize the President to impose tariffs.<Cite s="scotus-24-1287" passage="Held: IEEPA does not authorize the President to impose tariffs." /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">USTR the same day said alternative tools would be implemented. It named a temporary surcharge under Section 122 of the Trade Act of 1974, new Section 301 investigations, and existing Section 232 tariffs. It did not name Section 338.<Cite s="ustr-ieepa-2026-02-20" passage="alternative tools would be implemented" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The July 20 proclamations used Section 338. The saved statute text carries a duty power and an exclusion power: such products "shall be excluded from importation."<Cite s="usc-19-1338" passage="shall be excluded from importation" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">BBC converted the Canadian figure the day Finance announced it, as {bbcCovered}.<Cite s="bbc-canada-tariffs-2026-08-25" passage="nearly C$28bn ($20bn; £15bn)" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The Associated Press later used "about $20 billion" against the Census export total in U.S. dollars.<Cite s="ap-canada-tariffs-2026-09-08" passage="about $20 billion in American goods" /></p>

        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>Finance Canada's August 26 table, by rate</strong><span>Tariff items counted from the saved product list.</span></figcaption>
          <BarChart bars={rateBars} ariaLabel="Tariff items on the Finance Canada list by rate" />
          <p class="chart-source">{listRate50} lines at 50 percent, {listRate25} at 25 percent, {listRate15} at 15 percent, {listItems} in all.<Cite s="finance-product-list-2026-08-26" passage="List updated as of August 26, 2026" /></p>
          <p class="chart-vintage" data-layer="proof">Counted from the Finance Canada table saved August 26, 2026. The August 25 archive copy has {listItemsAug25} lines.</p>
        </figure>

        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>The Finance list is the two orders, line for line</strong><span>Canadian tariff items counted from the English schedules of PC 2026-0785 and PC 2026-0786 and from the Finance August 26 HTML table. U.S. July 20 Annex I counted as unique HTSUS 8-digit lines, a different unit.</span></figcaption>
          <DataTable columns={["Count", "What it is", "How it was counted"]} rows={orderCountRows} />
          <p class="chart-source">{oic0785Items} plus {oic0786Items} is {oicUnionItems}, the same unique-code total as the Finance table, with {oicOverlap} overlap and {financeRateMismatches} rate mismatches.<Cite s="oic-pc-2026-0785" passage="Goods Subject to 15% Surtax" /><Cite s="oic-pc-2026-0786" passage="Goods Subject to 25% Surtax" /></p>
          <p class="chart-vintage" data-layer="proof">English schedules only: PC 2026-0785 file offsets 7090 to 11003 for Schedules 1 to 3; PC 2026-0786 file offsets 3038 to 9151 for Schedules 1, 1.1, 2, and 2.1. Finance rates taken from the last column of each HTML table row.</p>
        </figure>

        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>The coverage dollars against the Census 2025 export total</strong><span>Finance Canada's C${financeCoveredCad} billion converts to about US${computed.usdCoveredRounded} billion at the August 25 Bank of Canada rate. Census totals are in U.S. dollars.</span></figcaption>
          <DataTable columns={["Figure", "What it is", "Record"]} rows={moneyRows} />
          <p class="chart-source">Twenty billion is {computed.share20OfUsExportsPct} percent of the Census 2025 export total.<Cite s="census-c1220-canada" passage="333,619.7" /></p>
          <p class="chart-vintage" data-layer="proof">Census annual totals taken from the saved goods-with-Canada table. Finance figures from the August 25 release. Rate from Bank of Canada valet FXUSDCAD for August 25, 2026.</p>
        </figure>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The {listItems} lines on Finance's public list are not a different unit from the Orders. They are the {oic0785Items} codes in PC 2026-0785 plus the {oic0786Items} steel and aluminum codes in PC 2026-0786. A reader who stops at the 2026 surtax order sees the 15, 25, and 50 percent consumer and machinery lines and misses the steel and aluminum increase that comes into force with it. The U.S. July 20 Annex I files, counted the same way, list {annexJulyItems} unique HTSUS 8-digit lines ({annexJulyDairyItems} dairy, {annexJulyAlcoholItems} alcohol, {annexJulyMotorItems} motor-vehicles basket). That is a different unit from a Canadian tariff item.<Cite s="oic-pc-2026-0786" passage="Schedules 1 and 2 to the Order are replaced by the Schedules 1 to 2.1 set out in the schedule to this Order" /><Cite s="annex-i-dairy-2026-07-20" passage="A 50% ad valorem Section 338 tariff shall apply" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Even at Finance's C${financeCoveredCad} billion, this is a slice of a goods relationship the Census Bureau puts above US$700 billion a year, two ways. The rates on the listed lines are not a slice. They are 15, 25, or 50 percent of the value of those lines.<Cite s="ustr-canada" passage="U.S. goods trade (exports plus imports) with Canada totaled an estimated $715.5 billion in 2025" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">A year earlier, on September 1, 2025, Canada removed surtaxes on ${rollbackConsumer2025} billion of consumer goods and ${rollbackOther2025} billion of other goods. The Gazette cited supply chains and consumer prices. The 2026 order is a partial return to broad counter-tariffs after that removal.<Cite s="gazette-sor-2025-181" passage="this Order comes into force on September 1, 2025" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The Canada-United States-Mexico Agreement, CUSMA, is the trade pact that replaced NAFTA on July 1, 2020. CBSA Customs Notice 26-23 says the surtax applies to goods "eligible to be marked as goods of the U.S. in accordance with the Determination of Country of Origin for the Purpose of Marking Goods (CUSMA Countries) Regulations."<Cite s="cbsa-cn26-23" passage="Determination of Country of Origin for the Purpose of Marking Goods (CUSMA Countries) Regulations" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Global Affairs Canada's joint-review page says the first mandatory review of the agreement "must take place on the 6th anniversary of the Agreement’s entry into force, which is July 1, 2026," and that the agreement "remains in force until 2036." The department's May 2026 monthly trade report, which records that meeting, says Canada and Mexico confirmed they wanted the pact renewed for another 16 years. The United States did not agree. The three countries now review the agreement every year, and it stays in force for another 10 years. The saved records do not say that outcome caused the July 20 proclamations. It sits before them on the timeline.<Cite s="cusma-joint-review" passage="The first Joint Review must take place on the 6th anniversary of the Agreement’s entry into force, which is July 1, 2026" /><Cite s="gac-monthly-2026-05" passage="The United States did not agree to renew the Agreement at this time" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="who-feels-it">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">A dated list, then who pays</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Who feels it</h2>

        <ul class="dated-list" data-layer="fact">
          <li>August 22, 2026: Carney said some U.S. companies and states "are innocent bystanders in a dispute they did not want," and that the Canadian step "will raise costs and reduce choice for Canadians."<Cite s="carney-remarks-2026-08-22" passage="innocent bystanders in a dispute they did not want" /></li>
          <li>August 24: Ontario's newsroom page for a premier's-office release on broadening the Protect Ontario Financing Program was saved, but the saved file is a JavaScript shell. The readable text is the page title only.<Cite s="ontario-ford-2026-08-24" passage="Ontario Newsroom" /></li>
          <li>August 25: Finance named steel, dairy, appliances, agricultural equipment, pulp and paper, and electronics as the focus, and said 50 percent would apply to steel and aluminum that had been at 25 percent, plus furniture and clothing.<Cite s="finance-countermeasures-2026-08-25" passage="steel, dairy, appliances, agricultural equipment, pulp and paper, and electronics" /></li>
          <li>August 25: BBC named cotton T-shirts and fresh tuna on the Canadian list, and wrote that Trump had threatened to hike U.S. tariffs on Canadian automobiles to 50 percent as of January 1.<Cite s="bbc-canada-tariffs-2026-08-25" passage="hike US tariffs on Canadian automobiles to 50% as of 1 January" /></li>
          <li>August 26: the saved table includes U.S. milk powders at 50 percent, specified cheeses at 25 percent, air-conditioning machines at 15 percent (8415.10.00), perfumes at 50 percent, cotton T-shirts at 50 percent, and smartphones at 50 percent.<Cite s="finance-product-list-2026-08-26" passage="8517.13.00 | Telephone sets, including smartphones" /></li>
          <li>August 26: Finance's complete-list page, updated the same day, calls itself "the authoritative source for the complete list."<Cite s="finance-complete-list" passage="This page remains the authoritative source for the complete list" /></li>
          <li>September 2: the Bank of Canada held its overnight rate at 2.25 percent and wrote that "New US tariffs and Canadian counter-tariffs will also raise costs for some businesses and could feed into consumer prices over time."<Cite s="boc-fad-2026-09-02" passage="New US tariffs and Canadian counter-tariffs will also raise costs for some businesses and could feed into consumer prices over time" /></li>
          <li>September 7: CBSA said the surtax applies to commercial and casual imports of U.S.-origin goods, including shipments under de minimis thresholds, and that in-transit goods that day are excepted with proof.<Cite s="cbsa-cn26-23" passage="Surtax is applicable on shipments that fall under de minimis thresholds" /></li>
          <li>September 7: the government of Quebec announced measures "afin d'atténuer le plus possible les impacts sur le Québec des nouveaux droits de douane," including reserving some public tenders for firms with an establishment in Quebec or Canada, and said Canada would reply with counter-tariffs on September 8.<Cite s="quebec-frechette-2026-09-07" passage="Le Canada répliquera avec des contre-tarifs le 8 septembre" /></li>
          <li>September 8: Carney, in the video transcribed by the Winnipeg Free Press, named autos, forestry, steel, and aluminum as the sectors hit hardest by the U.S. duties, and said the ${supportPackage} billion is "on top of nearly $25 billion already delivered."<Cite s="wfp-carney-forward-guidance-2026-09-08" passage="targeted help for the sectors hit hardest: autos, forestry, steel, aluminum" /></li>
          <li>September 8, dairy exclusion, from September 29: the annex bars Canadian whey, molasses, and non-alcoholic beer. Canadian whey producers lose the U.S. market for those lines.<Cite s="annex-i-dairy-2026-09-08" passage="Whey protein concentrates" /></li>
          <li>September 8, motor-vehicles exclusion, from September 29: the annex bars Canadian motorcycles, including mopeds, with an engine over 800 cc. Canadian makers of those motorcycles lose the U.S. market for that line.<Cite s="annex-i-motor-vehicles-2026-09-08" passage="Motorcycles (including mopeds)" /></li>
          <li>September 8, alcohol exclusion, from September 29: the annex bars packaged Canadian beer, sparkling and still grape wine, cider, sake, vermouth, and other listed fermented drinks. Canadian brewers and wineries of those packaged lines lose the U.S. market.<Cite s="annex-i-alcohol-2026-09-08" passage="Beer made from malt" /></li>
          <li>September 8, motor-vehicles scope change, from September 15: Annex I Part A adds {annexAutoAddItems} lines at 50 percent, including golf carts, small spark-ignition passenger vehicles, outboard motorboats, seats, and furniture. Annex I Part B removes {annexAutoRemoveItems} lines from that duty, including salt, Portland cement, refined lead, and fishing-rod parts.<Cite s="annex-i-auto-scope-2026-09-08" passage="New Products Covered by Section 338 Tariffs" /></li>
          <li>September 8, alcoholic-beverages scope change, from September 15: Annex I Part A adds {annexAlcoholScopeAddItems} lines at 50 percent, including specified cheeses, chemically modified fats, hides, furskins, and motorboats. Annex I Part B removes {annexAlcoholScopeRemoveItems} lines from that duty: whiskies and liqueurs in containers over 4 liters.<Cite s="annex-i-alcohol-scope-2026-09-08" passage="New Products Covered by Section 338 Tariffs" /></li>
        </ul>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The importer, the person who brings listed U.S. goods into Canada, is who must declare the surtax and enter the amount owing. Perfume, a cotton T-shirt, a phone, cheese, and a window air conditioner are on the Finance table.<Cite s="cbsa-cn26-23" passage="Importers must declare imported goods as subject to a surtax" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The Bank of Canada's 2018 staff note found "high but incomplete pass-through of tariffs to consumer goods prices." That is a finding from a prior episode.<Cite s="boc-san-2025-18" passage="high but incomplete pass-through of tariffs to consumer goods prices" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The Bank's April 2025 projection scenarios "assume that 75% of the increased costs from tariffs is passed on to consumer prices within six quarters." That is an assumption, not the 2018 finding.<Cite s="boc-san-2025-18" passage="assume that 75% of the increased costs from tariffs is passed on to consumer prices within six quarters" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The September 2 decision said new U.S. tariffs and Canadian counter-tariffs "could feed into consumer prices over time." That pass-through is conditional in the Bank's words, not a measured household price on this page.<Cite s="boc-fad-2026-09-02" passage="could feed into consumer prices over time" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Who is exempt or gets the charge back. The order's sections 1 and 2 do not charge Chapter 98 and 99 goods, goods in transit, qualifying Campobello Island imports, and goods under the Import for Re-Export Program.<Cite s="oic-pc-2026-0785" passage="goods that are classified under a tariff item of Chapter 98 or 99" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Sections 3 to 8 grant remission of surtax "paid or payable" for public health, public safety, national security, and goods used in manufacturing, processing, agricultural production, and food and beverage packaging.<Cite s="oic-pc-2026-0785" passage="remission is granted of surtaxes paid or payable" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Customs Notice 26-23 remits the surtax for goods under the Akwesasne Residents Remission Order. Postal and courier remission orders still leave the surtax due, and it must be accounted for. Duties relief and drawback remain available.<Cite s="cbsa-cn26-23" passage="The surtax is remitted for commercial and casual goods eligible for remission under the Akwesasne Residents Remission Order" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Finance's C${financeCoveredCad} billion is the gross basket of listed trade, not the amount that will be assessed or collected.<Cite s="finance-countermeasures-2026-08-25" passage="products covering $27.6 billion in imports from the U.S." /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Al Jazeera wrote that "Canada is the largest buyer of US-manufactured cars." This page does not hold a Census or Commerce series that ranks buyers of U.S.-made cars.<Cite s="aljazeera-canada-tariffs-2026-09-08" passage="Canada is the largest buyer of US-manufactured cars" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Existing Canadian auto surtaxes "remain in place," Finance said.<Cite s="finance-countermeasures-2026-08-25" passage="including autos, remain in place" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">A separate Gazette remission order still writes off some vehicle surtax for importers who keep producing in Canada.<Cite s="gazette-si-2026-13-motor-remission" passage="United States Surtax Remission Order (Motor Vehicles 2026)" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The costs are uneven. Carney said the U.S. tariffs "will hurt some Canadians more than others, and that was by design."<Cite s="wfp-carney-forward-guidance-2026-09-08" passage="hurt some Canadians more than others, and that was by design" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The Canadian list then points the Canadian charge at U.S. consumer goods a shopper would recognize. The support package is built for firms and workers in the sectors the U.S. already hit.<Cite s="finance-countermeasures-2026-08-25" passage="steel, dairy, appliances, agricultural equipment, pulp and paper, and electronics" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The September 29 U.S. bans hit Canadian whey, motorcycle, and packaged-alcohol exporters.<Cite s="annex-i-dairy-2026-09-08" passage="Whey protein concentrates" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="outlets">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Errors first, then what checked out</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What the coverage got right, and what it got wrong</h2>
        <p data-layer="fact">Thirteen outlets were checked against the primary records. Nine independent reporting lineages plus one transcript carrier, and fifteen cards below, including a coverage-style row for the September 8 White House fact sheet. PBS carried the Associated Press Toronto dispatch and is not a second lineage. MarketScreener, the Detroit News, and Investing.com carried Reuters. The Winnipeg Free Press carried a transcript of Carney's September 8 video. Errors first.</p>

        <div data-layer="fact">
        <OutletCheck outlet="Winnipeg Free Press" claim={`transcribed Carney saying new ports, mines, and energy corridors &ldquo;$500 billion in new private investment.&rdquo;`} verdict={`The Major Projects Office lists $192 billion in new investment and $500 billion in future private sector investment. The video uses new for the office's future figure.`} chip="mislabeled" status="warn"><Cite s="wfp-carney-forward-guidance-2026-09-08" passage="$500 billion in new private investment" /><Cite s="mpo-projects-transformative-strategies" passage="$192 billion in new investment" /></OutletCheck>

        <OutletCheck outlet="Winnipeg Free Press" claim={`transcribed Carney saying the Marconi SAFE contract was announced &ldquo;In July of this year.&rdquo;`} verdict={`The Prime Minister's readout of that announcement is dated June 15, 2026. The video's McKinley tariff history is the video's characterization, unchecked here.`} chip="mislabeled" status="warn"><Cite s="wfp-carney-forward-guidance-2026-09-08" passage="In July of this year" /><Cite s="pm-readout-marconi-2026-06-15" passage="Marconi Technologies has been awarded a contract exceeding $10 million" /></OutletCheck>

        <OutletCheck outlet="MarketScreener (Reuters)" claim={`wrote that the counter-tariffs impose duties &ldquo;across around 700 products imported from south of the border, a government statement said.&rdquo;`} verdict={`No saved government statement, table, or CBSA notice prints 700. The August 25 Finance table has ${listItemsAug25} tariff items. Unconfirmed.`} chip="unconfirmed" status="warn"><Cite s="marketscreener-reuters-2026-08-25" passage="across around 700 products imported from south of the border, a government statement said" /></OutletCheck>

        <OutletCheck outlet="Associated Press" claim={`wrote that Section 338 tariff authority &ldquo;has never been used, let alone tested in court,&rdquo; and characterized the administration as having &ldquo;made no attempt to calculate the dollar amount of damage.&rdquo;`} verdict={`The never-used half checks out against CRS, which wrote in February 2025 that the United States had never imposed tariffs under Section 338. No court ruling on a Section 338 duty is saved. The no-attempt-to-calculate line is AP's characterization, not a finding in the proclamations.`} chip="checks out" status="ok"><Cite s="ap-section-338-2026-08-29" passage="has never been used, let alone tested in court" /><Cite s="crs-r48435" passage="As of the time of this writing, the United States has never imposed tariffs under" /><Cite s="ap-section-338-2026-08-29" passage="made no attempt to calculate the dollar amount of damage" /></OutletCheck>

        <OutletCheck outlet="USA Today" claim={`wrote that the U.S. imposed 50 percent tariffs on &ldquo;an estimated 5% worth of Canadian imports,&rdquo; and that Canada's reply covers &ldquo;about $20 billion worth of U.S. products.&rdquo;`} verdict={`USTR on July 20 valued the three Section 338 actions at nearly US$20 billion of imports from Canada. That figure is ${computed.share20OfUsImportsPct} percent of the Census 2025 U.S. import total from Canada (US$${computed.censusImports2025Rounded} billion). Estimated 5 percent is consistent with that arithmetic.`} chip="consistent" status="ok"><Cite s="usatoday-canada-tariffs-2026-09-08" passage="estimated 5% worth of Canadian imports" /></OutletCheck>

        <OutletCheck outlet="The Hill" claim={`wrote that the tariffs cover &ldquo;more than 700 U.S. products&rdquo; and &ldquo;around 6 percent of what the U.S. exported to Canada last year,&rdquo; next to a &ldquo;$27.6 billion&rdquo; lead.`} verdict={`Six percent is US$20 billion against the Census 2025 export total of US$${computed.censusExports2025Rounded} billion: that arithmetic checks out. The 700-product count is unconfirmed. The saved Finance tables have ${listItemsAug25} tariff items on August 25 and ${listItems} on August 26, not 700 products.`} chip="checks out" status="ok"><Cite s="hill-canada-tariffs-2026-09-08" passage="around 6 percent of what the U.S. exported to Canada last year" /></OutletCheck>

        <OutletCheck outlet="Al Jazeera" claim={`wrote that Canada &ldquo;would launch a $5.42bn support package&rdquo; and that the tariffs apply to &ldquo;nearly $20bn worth of US imports&rdquo; and &ldquo;more than 700 products.&rdquo;`} verdict={`US$${computed.usdSupport} billion is C$${supportPackage} billion divided by the Bank of Canada August 25 rate of ${fxUsdCad20260825}: consistent. The 700-product count is unconfirmed.`} chip="consistent" status="ok"><Cite s="aljazeera-canada-tariffs-2026-09-08" passage="$5.42bn support package" /></OutletCheck>

        <OutletCheck outlet="The Detroit News (Reuters)" claim={`carried the Reuters dispatch converting Finance as &ldquo;C$27.6 billion ($19.94 billion)&rdquo; of U.S. goods, and the same 700-product line as MarketScreener.`} verdict={`C$${financeCoveredCad} billion at the Bank of Canada August 25 rate is US$${computed.usdCovered} billion: consistent. The text is Reuters, as MarketScreener carried it. The 700-product attribution is the same unconfirmed count.`} chip="consistent" status="ok"><Cite s="detroitnews-canada-tariffs-2026-08-25" passage="C$27.6 billion ($19.94 billion)" /></OutletCheck>

        <OutletCheck outlet="BBC" claim={`wrote that the Canadian tariffs apply to &ldquo;nearly C$28bn ($20bn; £15bn)&rdquo; and that support programmes total &ldquo;C$7.5bn.&rdquo;`} verdict={`Finance Canada prints ${financeCoveredCad} billion and ${supportPackage} billion with no currency tag. The C$ conversion and the US$20 billion equivalent match the Bank of Canada rate as rounded.`} chip="consistent" status="ok"><Cite s="bbc-canada-tariffs-2026-08-25" passage="nearly C$28bn ($20bn; £15bn)" /></OutletCheck>

        <OutletCheck outlet="The Guardian" claim={`wrote that the tariffs apply to products covering &ldquo;C$27.6bn in imports from the US&rdquo; and quoted the government that they do not apply to goods in transit.`} verdict="The C$ label matches how Finance Canada writes. The in-transit exception is in the Order and in Customs Notice 26-23." chip="consistent" status="ok"><Cite s="guardian-canada-tariffs-2026-09-07" passage="C$27.6bn in imports from the US" /></OutletCheck>

        <OutletCheck outlet="CNBC" claim={`wrote that the duties range from 15% to 50% across hundreds of U.S. products &ldquo;worth a total $27.6 billion.&rdquo;`} verdict={`Finance Canada prints ${financeCoveredCad} billion. CNBC does not tag the currency. The figure is the same unlabeled total.`} chip="consistent" status="ok"><Cite s="cnbc-canada-tariffs-2026-09-08" passage="worth a total $27.6 billion" /></OutletCheck>

        <OutletCheck outlet="CBC News" claim={`wrote that Finance Canada dropped seafood and fish products from the list and that the decision was based on &ldquo;feedback.&rdquo;`} verdict={`The August 26 table has none of the ${removedCodes} chapter 03 lines that were on the August 25 table, and it adds the nine industrial lines CBC named. The feedback reason is what CBC reported Finance said. It is not a line on either saved table.`} chip="checks out" status="ok"><Cite s="cbc-seafood-tariffs-2026-08-27" passage={`the decision was based on "feedback."`} /><Cite s="finance-product-list-2026-08-26" passage="4402.90.10 | Wood charcoal" /></OutletCheck>

        <OutletCheck outlet="Reuters, as carried by Investing.com" claim={`wrote that Canada removed seafood and fish products from the counter-tariff list, quoting Finance: &ldquo;including removing seafood and fish products from our list of counter tariffs.&rdquo;`} verdict={`The saved tables match the removal. Reuters.com does not let its pages be saved; this card uses the Investing.com carrier. The reason is what Reuters reported Finance posted, not a government record held as its own file.`} chip="checks out" status="ok"><Cite s="investing-reuters-seafood-2026-08-27" passage="including removing seafood and fish products from our list of counter tariffs" /></OutletCheck>

        <OutletCheck outlet="White House fact sheet, September 8" claim={`wrote that Canada imposed new retaliatory tariffs on &ldquo;about $20 billion of U.S. exports, including steel, dairy, and agricultural equipment.&rdquo;`} verdict={`Finance printed C$${financeCoveredCad} billion of imports from the U.S., about US$${computed.usdCoveredRounded} billion at the August 25 Bank of Canada rate. Steel, dairy, and agricultural equipment are on the saved list. The fact sheet does not print the Canadian-dollar figure.`} chip="consistent" status="ok"><Cite s="wh-fact-sheet-2026-09-08" passage="about $20 billion of U.S. exports" /><Cite s="finance-countermeasures-2026-08-25" passage="products covering $27.6 billion in imports from the U.S." /></OutletCheck>
        <OutletCheckFold count={1} method="the Census export series and the Finance table.">
          <OutletCheck folded outlet="Associated Press" claim={`wrote that the Canadian tariffs &ldquo;cover about $20 billion in American goods, roughly 6% of the $333.6 billion the United States exported to Canada last year.&rdquo; PBS NewsHour carried the same dispatch.`} verdict={`The Census 2025 export total is ${census2025.exports.toLocaleString("en-US")} million dollars, US$${computed.censusExports2025Rounded} billion as rounded. Twenty billion is ${computed.share20OfUsExportsPct} percent of that total. The arithmetic checks out on the Census series.`} chip="checks out" status="ok"><Cite s="ap-canada-tariffs-2026-09-08" passage="roughly 6% of the $333.6 billion" /></OutletCheck>
        </OutletCheckFold>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="claim-check">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Dollar for dollar, rate for rate</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Claim checked against the record</h2>

        <div data-layer="fact">
          <CheckedBlock label="Canada matched the U.S. action dollar for dollar" variant="contested">
            <p>Finance Canada assigns C${financeCoveredCad} billion of covered trade to each side. Covered trade is the value of listed imports, not duty liability, revenue collected, trade displaced, or economic harm. Those are five different quantities. Finance's figure is the first.<Cite s="finance-countermeasures-2026-08-25" passage="products covering $27.6 billion in imports from the U.S." /></p>
            <p>The saved records do not reproduce that valuation by tariff code and reference period. The figure did not change when {removedCodes} seafood lines left the list. No saved record explains why. See the first catch row.<Cite s="finance-product-list-2026-08-26" passage="List updated as of August 26, 2026" /></p>
            <p>Finance also says each Canadian rate is "based on the matching U.S. rate for the same goods." This page compared Finance's rate column with the Canadian orders. That proves the table matches Canadian law. It does not prove each Canadian rate matches the U.S. rate on the same goods. No saved record gives a product-level crosswalk.<Cite s="finance-product-list-2026-08-26" passage="based on the matching U.S. rate for the same goods" /></p>
            <p>Champagne's quoted line is "dollar-for-dollar, rate for rate." Both halves are Finance's characterizations. The White House fact sheet does not print a matching dollar total. BBC converted the Canadian side as {bbcCovered}.<Cite s="finance-countermeasures-2026-08-25" passage="dollar-for-dollar, rate for rate" /></p>
          </CheckedBlock>
        </div>

        <details class="receipt" data-layer="proof"><summary>how the 6 percent and the conversions were counted</summary>
          <p>Spring Economic Update 2026 intro: approximately {springUpdateTariffFreePct} percent of goods trade tariff-free; chart 0.7 Average Tariff Rate Canada {springUpdateAvgTariffCanada} Global {springUpdateAvgTariffGlobal}. Gazette SOR/2025-181: ${rollbackConsumer2025} billion consumer goods and ${rollbackOther2025} billion other goods removed from September 1, 2025. Census c1220 TOTAL 2025 exports 333,619.7 million U.S. dollars. 20,000 / 333,619.7 = 0.05995, {computed.share20OfUsExportsPct} percent as rounded in the story. Census c1220 TOTAL 2025 imports 381,922.1 million. 20,000 / 381,922.1 = 0.05236, {computed.share20OfUsImportsPct} percent. Bank of Canada FXUSDCAD August 25 = {fxUsdCad20260825}. {financeCoveredCad} / {fxUsdCad20260825} = {computed.usdCovered}. {supportPackage} / {fxUsdCad20260825} = {computed.usdSupport}. The 17 days are 2026-09-08 minus 2026-08-22. The 33 days are 2026-08-22 minus 2026-07-20. The original Section 338 clock is 2026-08-19 minus 2026-07-20 = {daysJulyProcToOriginalEffective} days. The September additions are 2026-09-15 minus 2026-09-08 = {daysSeptModifyToEffective} days. PC 2026-0785 English Schedules 1 to 3: {oic0785Schedule1} + {oic0785Schedule2} + {oic0785Schedule3} = {oic0785Items}. PC 2026-0786 English Schedules 1, 1.1, 2, 2.1: {oic0786Schedule1} + {oic0786Schedule11} + {oic0786Schedule2} + {oic0786Schedule21} = {oic0786Items}. Overlap {oicOverlap}. Union {oic0785Items} + {oic0786Items} = {oicUnionItems}, matching the Finance August 26 HTML table of {listItems} unique codes. Finance last-column rates versus those schedules: {financeRateMismatches} mismatches. Support package 1.5 + 0.5 + 2 + 3.5 = {computed.supportSum}. August 25 unique codes {listItemsAug25} minus {removedCodes} chapter 03 plus {addedCodes} added = {listItems}. July 20 Annex I unique HTSUS 8-digit lines: {annexJulyDairyItems} + {annexJulyAlcoholItems} + {annexJulyMotorItems} = {annexJulyItems}. September 8 annex counts are HTSUS product-code lines, including {annexAlcoholScopeAddItems} added and {annexAlcoholScopeRemoveItems} removed on the alcohol-scope annex.</p>
        </details>
      </section>

      <section id="what-happened-next">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The same Tuesday in Washington</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened next</h2>

        <p data-layer="narrative">On September 8 the White House issued proclamations that exclude certain Canadian products from importation, effective 12:01 a.m. eastern on September 29: one tied to dairy, one tied to motor vehicles, and one tied to alcoholic beverages. A further proclamation modifies the motor-vehicles basket from September 15. Another of the same day modifies the alcoholic-beverages basket from September 15: {annexAlcoholScopeAddItems} HTSUS lines added at 50 percent, {annexAlcoholScopeRemoveItems} removed.<Cite s="proc-modify-alcohol-2026-09-08" passage="12:01 a.m. eastern time on September 15, 2026" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The White House fact sheet of September 8 says the President directed the U.S. Trade Representative and the Administrator of the General Services Administration to remove US${gsaDirectedUsd} billion of Canadian-origin products from GSA's Multiple Award Schedules, the catalogues federal agencies buy from. No saved record shows a removal carried out. Al Jazeera quoted a Truth Social post with a similar direction. This page does not hold that post as its own saved file.<Cite s="wh-fact-sheet-2026-09-08" passage="remove $50 billion dollars" /><Cite s="aljazeera-canada-tariffs-2026-09-08" passage="REMOVE Canadian-origin products from GSA’s Multiple Award Schedules" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Al Jazeera also wrote that Trump threatened to block Bombardier from selling planes in the United States unless it began manufacturing them there, and that Bombardier said it employs thousands of workers in the United States.<Cite s="aljazeera-canada-tariffs-2026-09-08" passage="block Canada-based aircraft manufacturer Bombardier" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Watching: whether CBSA publishes a later notice that changes the September 8 list, whether the September 15 motor-vehicles-basket change and the September 29 U.S. import exclusions are carried out as written, and whether formal talks resume. Gabriel Brunet, a spokesman for Dominic LeBlanc, Minister responsible for Canada-U.S. Trade, told AP that officials "remain in contact even though formal talks have not resumed."<Cite s="ap-canada-tariffs-2026-09-08" passage="remain in contact even though formal talks have not resumed" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="unknowns">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">What the records do not hold</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What we do not know yet</h2>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The White House fact sheet does not state a dollar value for the goods covered by the three July 20 proclamations. USTR's July 20 statement does: nearly US$20 billion in imports from Canada. BBC's conversion and AP's later $20 billion against the Census export total sit next to that USTR figure, not on the fact sheet.<Cite s="ustr-section-338-2026-07-20" passage="nearly $20 billion in imports from Canada" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Al Jazeera attributed a Tuesday remark to U.S. Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent. This page does not hold a Treasury or Commerce transcript of that remark.<Cite s="aljazeera-canada-tariffs-2026-09-08" passage="US Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Carney's September 8 words here are the Winnipeg Free Press text of the YouTube transcript. Capture of the official YouTube address saved the watch page titled Forward Guidance: A Stronger Canada. It did not save the video file.<Cite s="carney-forward-guidance-youtube-2026-09-08" passage="Forward Guidance: A Stronger Canada" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The dairy-ban proclamation of September 8 refers to a same-day proclamation modifying the dairy basket.<Cite s="proc-exclude-dairy-2026-09-08" passage="Modifying the Scope of Products of Canada Subject to the Additional Duties Imposed To Offset Canadian Discrimination Against the Commerce of the United States With Respect to Dairy" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The White House presidential-actions listing was searched again on September 9. It lists the alcohol-scope change and the dairy exclusion. It does not list a dairy-scope change.<Cite s="wh-presidential-actions-listing-2026-09-09" passage="Excluding Certain Canadian Products from Importation into the United States in Response to Continued Discrimination Against the Commerce of the United States with Respect to Dairy" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Two URLs built on the alcohol-scope and motor-vehicles-scope patterns were requested through the same capture route and were not saved pages. The Federal Register public-inspection current page saved the same day is a prototype notice. It does not list a dairy-scope document.<Cite s="fr-public-inspection-2026-09-09" passage="Federal Register Documents Currently on Public Inspection" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Finance assigns C${financeCoveredCad} billion of covered trade to each side. The import-value calculation by tariff code and reference period is not a saved record.<Cite s="finance-sarnia-2026-08-27" passage="products covering $27.6 billion in imports from the U.S." /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">A search of Finance news for August 25 to 28, 2026, found the August 27 Sarnia release, which repeats the figure and does not name a method. The August 2026 news index was not a saved page.<Cite s="finance-news-landing-2026-09-09" passage="Find the latest news and communications products from Department of Finance Canada." /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">No saved record maps each Canadian tariff item to a U.S. Section 338 or Section 232 rate on the same goods.<Cite s="finance-product-list-2026-08-26" passage="based on the matching U.S. rate for the same goods" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">PC 2026-0785 says if it is registered after September 8 it comes into force on the registration day. CBSA Notice 26-23 says September 8. No registration receipt is saved.<Cite s="oic-pc-2026-0785" passage="if it is registered after that day, it comes into force on the day on which it is registered" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">No saved CARM assessment, remission total, drawback, or net-collection figure is on this page. C${financeCoveredCad} billion is the gross basket, not collections.<Cite s="finance-countermeasures-2026-08-25" passage="products covering $27.6 billion in imports from the U.S." /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Ontario's August 24 premier's-office release on tariff-impacted workers was captured, but the Ontario newsroom served a JavaScript shell. The saved text is the page title. A readable premier statement from that page is absent.<Cite s="ontario-ford-2026-08-24" passage="Ontario Newsroom" /></p>
        <p data-layer="fact">No saved Canadian court filing or World Trade Organization complaint on these September 8 measures is on this page.</p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Census Bureau state-level goods exports to Canada for 2025 are not in the saved files. The Census state page and the December 2025 origin-of-movement table were captured. That table is U.S. goods exports by state to all countries, not destination Canada. The Census International Trade API asked for a key and did not return the series.<Cite s="census-origin-movement-index" passage="Origin of Movement Exports, Origin state-based" /></p>
      </section>

